      <div type="clause" n="2">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:LeisWl">Leis Wl</ref> 2</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="xno" source="#liebermann" corresp="#wit-hk"><pb n="492–493" ed="#liebermann"/>E ki enfrei[n]t<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.1"/> pais le rei, en Merchenelahe cent souz les amendes. Autresi de hemfare<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.2"/> et de agwait purpensé.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="xno" source="#liebermann" corresp="#wit-i"><pb n="492–493" ed="#liebermann"/>E qui<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="24" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.24"/> enfraint<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="25" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.25"/> la pais le rei, en Merchenelae cent solz les amendes. Altresi de hemfare<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="26" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.26"/> et de aweit purpensed<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="27" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.27"/>.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" corresp="#wit-l"><pb n="492–493" ed="#liebermann"/>De pace regia. Qui pacem regiam infregerit, secundum Merchenalahe C solid. pene succumbet. Similis de hamfare et insidiis precogitatis pena delinquentem<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="d" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.d"/> manet.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="xno" source="#robertson" n="2" corresp="#wit-hk"><pb n="252" ed="#robertson"/>E ki enfrei[n]t pais le rei, en Merchenelahe cent souz les amendes. Autresi de hemfare et de agwait purpensé. Ice[z] plai[z] afierent a la curune le rei.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="492–493" ed="#liebermann"/>2. Und wer des Königs Sonderfriedensschutz [‘Mund’] bricht: im Mercier-Rechtsgebiet 100 Schill.<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.de.1"/> Busse. Ebenso bei gewaltsamer Heimsuchung<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.de.2"/> und vorbedachtem<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#LeisWl_2.lnote.de.3"/> Überfall aus Hinterhalt.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">2. And whoever breaks the king's special-peace protection [‘mund’]: in the Mercian legal district 100 shill. compensation. Likewise for forcible house-attack and premeditated assault from ambush.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-hk"><pb n="253" ed="#robertson"/>And he who violates the king's peace shall pay 100 shillings as compensation in the province of Mercia. And similarly for attacks on people's houses and for premeditated waylaying. These pleas belong to the King's Crown.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="493"><ref target="clause:IICn_12">II Cn 12</ref>; <ref target="clause:IICn_14">II Cn 14f.</ref>; <ref target="clause:ICn_3-2">I Cn 3.2</ref></note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">II Cn. 12; 14 f. | I Cn. 3, 2</note>
        <note type="editorial">Added October 2026 from Robertson pp. 252–253 (text and translation); Liebermann’s text and German translation added from Gesetze I p. 492–493, October 2026 (two independent readings of the page images)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">492</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">enfreit <hi rend="italic">Hk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">enfreit Hk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">492</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">hamf. <hi rend="italic">L;</hi> heinf. <hi rend="italic">bevorzugt Toynbee</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">hamf. L; heinf. preferred by Toynbee</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.24" n="24" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">492</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">que <hi rend="italic">Io Isp</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">que Io Isp</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.25" n="25" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">492</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">enfraiant <hi rend="italic">Isp;</hi> ensyaint <hi rend="italic">Io</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">enfraiant Isp; ensyaint Io</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.26" n="26" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">492</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">heinf. <hi rend="italic">Io Isp</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">heinf. Io Isp</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.27" n="27" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">492</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">prepensed <hi rend="italic">Im</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">prepensed Im</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.oe.d" n="d" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">493</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Römische Wendung, nicht aus Engl. Mittellatein</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Roman phrase, not from English Medieval Latin</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">286</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Wieder 5 £; <hi rend="italic">vgl. Gl</hi> Königsschutz 5—b</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Again 5 £; <hi rend="italic">cf. Gl</hi> Königsschutz 5—b</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">286</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Ebd</hi>. Heimsuchung 1b. 11; Verf. wählt das Normann. Wort; <hi rend="italic">heinfar praecogitata</hi> Domesday I 269.—Wilhelm I. trat <hi rend="italic">invasionibus</hi> entgegen (Wil. Pictav. ed. Giles 147): also im Kern authentisch</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Ibid</hi>. Heimsuchung 1b. 11; the author chooses the Norman word; <hi rend="italic">heinfar praecogitata</hi> Domesday I 269.—William I took action against <hi rend="italic">invasionibus</hi> (Wil. Pictav. ed. Giles 147): thus authentic in substance</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="LeisWl_2.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">286</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Rechtsperrung (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 4a. b. 6) steht in Agsächs. Recht an dieser Stelle; sie vermengt mit <hi rend="italic">assaltus</hi> auch Hn; der hier gewählte Französ. Terminus ist Normannisch; <hi rend="italic">vgl. Gl</hi> Angriff 6</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Obstruction (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 4a. b. 6) stands at this point in Anglo-Saxon law; Hn too confuses it with <hi rend="italic">assaltus</hi>; the French term chosen here is Norman; <hi rend="italic">cf. Gl</hi> Angriff 6</seg></note>
      </div>
